<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    TanMenu's design tokens — the ONE place a colour, radius, spacing step or type size is named.
    Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml consumes these and defines no literal of its own (ShellTokenTests
    enforces that).

    ── What this file became in Round 7, Tasks 7 and 9 ──────────────────────────────────────────
    It was the Fluent 2 palette: 46 keys of alpha-over-Mica neutrals and a Windows 11 type ramp.
    Task 7 executed the conversion design's §3.2 over all 46 — 9 became aliases onto the vendored
    Aero layer, 11 were re-valued, 23 were deleted and 3 kept — and added the 6 keys that section
    lists as new. Together with the 7 caption keys Task 1 added ahead of it that left Task 7 at
    36 keys, 22 themed and 14 variant-independent.

    TASK 9 THEN CUT ONE MORE and the census has to move with it: AccentDefaultBrush lost its last
    reader when the search field's accent underline went (the accent block below carries the full
    account), so this file declares 35 keys today — 21 themed and 14 variant-independent. Counted
    with comments stripped, the two ThemeDictionaries' own "Light"/"Dark" keys excluded.

    The values are Aero's; the NAMES are still ours, and that is the whole point of the seam. Aero's
    fills are two- and four-stop vertical gradients (Themes/Aero/AVMAP/Brushes.axaml); a token layer
    of flat SolidColorBrushes has no gradient to give, so anything that needs a STATEFUL Aero fill —
    a button's four states, a list row's hover — names the Aero brush directly from Launcher.axaml
    instead of going through a token here. ShellTokenTests' defined set unions the vendored files
    for exactly that reason.

    ── HOW AN ALIAS IS WRITTEN, AND WHY IT IS NOT <StaticResource> ──────────────────────────────
    The design's §3.1 writes an alias as <StaticResource x:Key="X" ResourceKey="AeroY" />, citing
    AVMAP/Metrics.axaml's own ControlContentThemeFontSize line as precedent. That form is used below
    for the two VARIANT-INDEPENDENT aliases and is NOT used for the seven themed ones, because the
    seven would resolve wrongly: every Aero brush lives inside a ThemeDictionaries block keyed
    "Light"/"Dark" (Themes/Aero/AeroResources.axaml), a StaticResource is resolved once against
    whichever variant happens to be live at parse time, and the resulting object would then be
    frozen into BOTH of this file's variant blocks.

    What is used instead is the mechanism the vendored brush file itself uses for all 110 of its own
    brushes: a SolidColorBrush whose Color is a {DynamicResource} onto the Aero COLOUR key that
    Variant/Light.axaml and Variant/Dark.axaml define. The value still lives in exactly one place —
    the two vendored variant files — and it follows a live 外观 change for free. The declaration is
    repeated verbatim in the Light and the Dark block below, which is the same arrangement
    AeroResources.axaml uses when it merges the one Brushes.axaml file into both dictionaries.

    ── Load order, unchanged ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    App.axaml lists FluentTheme, then Themes/Aero/AeroResources.axaml, then this file, then
    Launcher.axaml. Application.Styles is searched last-first (Avalonia.Styling.Styles.TryGetResource
    counts DOWN from Count-1, decompiled from Avalonia.Base 12.1.0), so a key redefined here beats
    both Aero's and Fluent's. The three keys at the bottom of this file are the ones this app
    overrides that way for Avalonia's own control templates.
  -->

  <Styles.Resources>
    <ResourceDictionary>

      <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

        <!-- ══ Light ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">

          <!-- ── Aliases onto the vendored Aero colour layer ──────────────────────────────────
               Each line names the Aero key its value comes from; none of them states a value.
               Light / Dark, read off Themes/Aero/Variant/*.axaml:

                 SurfaceBaseBrush      AeroWindowBackgroundColor       #FCFCFC / #373737
                 SurfacePageBrush      AeroListBoxBackgroundColor      #FFFFFF / #4A4A4A
                 TextPrimaryBrush      AeroForegroundColor             #000000 / #FFFFFF
                 TextDisabledBrush     AeroDisabledForegroundColor     #7F7F7F in BOTH
                 StrokeControlBrush    AeroGroupBoxBorderBrushColor    #D5DFE5 / #1B252B
                 StrokeDividerBrush    AeroGroupBoxBorderBrushColor    (a divider and a group-box
                                                                        outline are one hairline in
                                                                        a Windows 7 dialog)
                 FocusStrokeOuterBrush AeroForegroundColor             (the classic dotted focus
                                                                        rectangle, drawn in the ink
                                                                        colour — see the five
                                                                        FocusAdorners in
                                                                        Launcher.axaml)

               SurfaceBaseBrush has TWO consumers, not the one §3.2 predicted. Besides
               MainWindow.ApplyBackdrop's opaque fallback it now paints the window's CLIENT AREA —
               MainWindow.axaml's content Grid, everything below the caption band. That is Aero's own
               structure and it is what makes the 设置 page legible: glass is the FRAME, the client is
               opaque #FCFCFC. Left transparent over the acrylic, the page's ink sits on a live blur
               of whatever is behind the window; measured on this machine, the wallpaper averages
               (87,84,103), against which #003399 group headings reach 1.4:1 and black body text 2.9:1.
               MainWindow.axaml's own remark carries the arithmetic. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceBaseBrush"      Color="{DynamicResource AeroWindowBackgroundColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfacePageBrush"      Color="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxBackgroundColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextPrimaryBrush"      Color="{DynamicResource AeroForegroundColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextDisabledBrush"     Color="{DynamicResource AeroDisabledForegroundColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StrokeControlBrush"    Color="{DynamicResource AeroGroupBoxBorderBrushColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StrokeDividerBrush"    Color="{DynamicResource AeroGroupBoxBorderBrushColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FocusStrokeOuterBrush" Color="{DynamicResource AeroForegroundColor}" />

          <!-- The heading ink, for the 设置 page's five group headings, its page title and the 应用
               page's category headings. #003399 is PUBLISHED:
               https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-fonts gives "main
               instructions" as 12 pt blue #003399 and "group headers" as 11 pt #003399. Aero's one
               piece of text hierarchy was colour, not size, and this is it.

               THE DARK VALUE IS AUTHORED AND ITS DESIGN-DOCUMENT DERIVATION IS WRONG. §3.2 offers
               #6699FF as "#003399 lifted to keep the same contrast ratio against #373737 that the
               original has against #FCFCFC". Computed: #003399 on #FCFCFC is 10.2:1, #6699FF on
               #373737 is 4.3:1, so the stated criterion is not met — and it cannot be met by any
               recognisably blue colour, because 10.2:1 against #373737 needs a relative luminance of
               0.85, i.e. a near-white. The value below is the design's literal #6699FF, kept for
               that reason and not for the reason the design gave; what it actually delivers is 4.3:1,
               which clears WCAG's 3:1 large-text bar and misses the 4.5:1 body bar by 0.2.

               TASK 9 PUT THESE HEADINGS INSIDE AN AERO GROUP BOX AND THE NUMBER DID NOT MOVE, which
               is worth stating because Task 7 left a note here expecting it to. The vendored
               HeaderedContentControl theme sets Background="Transparent" and its header presenter
               sets it again, so a group head still sits on the window's own SurfaceBaseBrush and not
               on a surface of the box's. Re-measured on the shipped build, PrintWindow flag 2 at
               150 %, the 外观 head's extreme ink against the modal background of its own rect:
               ink (0,51,153) on (252,252,252) = 10.59:1 Light, ink (102,153,255) on (55,55,55) =
               4.29:1 Dark — the same two figures Task 7 recorded. A card title on the same capture
               reads 20.47:1 and 11.90:1. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HeadingBrush" Color="#003399" />

          <!-- Accent: design-time defaults only. Services/AppTheme.PublishAccent overwrites these in
               Application.Resources at startup with the user's real Windows accent. Round 7, Task 7
               cut what it publishes from five keys to three: AccentTextBrush had no consumer
               anywhere in the tree, and TextOnAccentBrush's only one was TmAccentButton, which
               Task 3 deleted with the accent-filled default button Windows 7 never had.

               ROUND 7, TASK 9 CUT THE THIRD, AccentDefaultBrush, and it is the last flat accent fill
               this app had. Task 7's own note here said it kept "two hairline consumers in
               Launcher.axaml — the rail's selection pill and the search field's focus underline —
               which Tasks 8 and 9 own": Task 8 deleted the pill with TmNavRowItem's template, Task 9
               deleted the underline (Border.tm-searchfield-focusbar) because Windows 7 signalled
               focus in a text field by repainting the field, not by drawing an accent bar under it.
               grep then found the key in this file, in AppTheme.Build and in four comments, and in no
               live lookup anywhere — so it stops being defined and stops being published.

               The accent has TWO jobs left and both are frame-or-identity rather than fill: the
               caption's glass tint (MainWindow.axaml) and the splash card's gradient
               (Controls/SplashPanel.axaml). Both take a raw Color, because a GradientStop does. Aero's
               user colour WAS DwmGetColorizationColor, a frame colour, so this is the role restored
               rather than a leftover — and with the brush gone, the two keys below are exactly those
               two jobs and nothing else. -->
          <Color x:Key="AccentColor">#0078D4</Color>
          <Color x:Key="AccentColorShaded">#00629F</Color>

          <!-- ══ The Aero caption (Round 7, Task 1; the Dark veil corrected by Task 7) ═════════
               MainWindow.axaml replaces Avalonia's WindowDrawnDecorations template wholesale and
               paints the caption itself. Everything the replacement reads is declared here so the
               band follows the 外观 setting like the rest of the app.

               THE GLASS IS TWO LAYERS, not one, and that is Aero's own structure rather than a
               shortcut: Aero glass was a live blur PLUS a colour tint bar PLUS a bright inner
               reflection. Avalonia's AcrylicBlur supplies the blur and nothing else (it is a plain
               gaussian blur with no tint layer at all), so the tint and the reflection are ours to
               draw. The tint is the ACCENT — AccentColor to AccentColorShaded, top to bottom, drawn
               inline in MainWindow.axaml because a GradientStop needs a live {DynamicResource
               AccentColor} and AppTheme republishes that key whenever Windows' personalization
               colour changes. The reflection is the veil below.

               THE VEIL'S SHAPE IS MEASURED; ITS ALPHAS ARE AUTHORED. Microsoft's own published
               Windows 7 screenshot
               (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/images/win-window-frames-image2.png,
               Calculator, 278x351, native resolution) has its caption band at y 14..43 and its
               brightest row 11-13 px below the band's top edge, darkening toward both ends — hence
               three stops at 0 / 0.4 / 1. The RGB values in that capture are deliberately NOT used:
               it is translucent glass over the green Windows 7 wallpaper, so every pixel is
               wallpaper-tinted, and Aero's caption colour was the user's colorization value over a
               blur rather than a fixed palette. The alphas 40 / 66 / 33 are AUTHORED.

               ── THE DARK VEIL WAS BLACK AND THAT WAS A DEFECT. ROUND 7 TASK 7 MADE IT WHITE. ───
               Task 1 implemented §3.2's "same structure, black-alpha" for Dark. A black veil at
               those alphas subtracts MOST at the 0.4 stop, so Aero's top highlight came out as a
               shadow: the band's extremum was a minimum, not a maximum, and the polarity was
               inverted against Light. MEASURED, PrintWindow flag 2 at 150 % scale, one column down
               the 45 px band at image x = 700, luminance 0.299R+0.587G+0.114B, three rows quoted
               (the band's top, the row Light peaks on, and the band's last row before the bevel):

                 LIGHT  before and after, byte-identical   y=0 89.5   y=17 120.0   y=40 81.4
                 DARK   before (black veil)                y=0 42.6   y=17  29.2   y=40 27.2
                 DARK   after  (white veil)                y=0 82.4   y=17  89.1   y=40 61.3

               Before, Dark fell monotonically from the band's top edge and reached its MINIMUM at
               y = 39-40; after, it rises to a MAXIMUM at y = 17 — the same row Light peaks on — and
               falls away on both sides. That is the polarity, and it is the whole of the fix.

               THE HALVED ALPHA IS AUTHORED and its job is to keep Dark dark: the peak measures 89.1
               against Light's 120.0, so the two variants are still visibly different bands rather
               than one band drawn twice. It costs the title contrast, and the cost is measured
               rather than waved through — the caption title over the band's own colour reads 16.16:1
               before and 6.79:1 after in Dark, against 4.56:1 before and 4.89:1 after in Light. The
               band got lighter and white-on-light is worth less; 6.79 still clears WCAG AA for body
               text and is the better of the two variants. Windows 7 had no dark mode at all — see
               the design document's decision 4 — so every Dark alpha here is an invention and says
               so.

               INACTIVE IS THE SAME GRADIENT AT HALF THE ACTIVE ALPHA, in both variants. AUTHORED.
               There is no active/inactive pseudo-class on WindowDrawnDecorations and no public path
               from the decorations to WindowBase.IsActive, so MainWindow.axaml.cs watches its own
               IsActive and writes the chosen brush into Window.Resources["CaptionGlassBrush"] — the
               same DynamicResource-republishing mechanism Services/AppTheme.cs uses for the accent.
               CaptionGlassBrush below is what the template resolves before that observer's first
               callback, and it is the INACTIVE gradient because a window is not active until it is
               shown. -->
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassActiveBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#40FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#66FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#33FFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassInactiveBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#20FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#33FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#1AFFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#20FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#33FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#1AFFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>

          <!-- The 2 DIP inner bevel along the band's bottom edge, LIGHT OVER DARK. MEASURED in the
               same capture: y = 42 is a light row and y = 43 a dark one, i.e. the last two rows of
               the 30 DIP band. Both alphas are AUTHORED — the capture's own values are
               wallpaper-tinted like everything else in that band, so only the light-over-dark
               ORDERING is taken from it.

               THIS PAIR'S POLARITY WAS ALREADY CORRECT IN BOTH VARIANTS and Task 7 did not touch
               either brush. MEASURED on the same column as the veil profile above, on the SHIPPED
               build: Light y=41-42 lum 168.2 over y=43-44 lum 56.9; Dark y=41-42 lum 108.9 over
               y=43-44 lum 34.4. Light line above dark line in both. (The Dark pair read 83.9 over
               16.0 before Task 7 lightened the band underneath them; the brushes are the same and
               only what they composite over moved.)

               THE DESIGN DOCUMENT'S PLAN FOR THE DARK LINE WAS TRIED AND MEASURED TO FAIL. Its §3.2
               makes CaptionBevelDarkBrush an alias onto the vendored AeroGroupBoxBorderBrush
               (#D5DFE5 Light / #1B252B Dark) so a group-box hairline and a caption bevel would be
               one key. Built that way and captured with PrintWindow in Light, the "dark" row read
               (213,223,229) against the light row's (186,184,190) — i.e. the dark line came out
               LIGHTER than the light one and the bevel was inverted, because #D5DFE5 is a pale
               border colour for a light SURFACE and this line sits over a mid-tone glass band. A
               black alpha is used instead and the alias is not adopted. Task 7 re-checked the
               question when it took StrokeControlBrush onto that same Aero key and reached the same
               answer: the group box moved, this line did not. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CaptionBevelLightBrush" Color="#80FFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CaptionBevelDarkBrush"  Color="#40000000" />

          <!-- The title's glow. This is the one hard Aero number Microsoft published and it maps
               exactly: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/dwm/customframe Appendix B
               draws caption text with DTT_COMPOSITED | DTT_GLOWSIZE and iGlowSize = 15, where
               iGlowSize is "the size of a glow that will be drawn on the background prior to any
               text being drawn". A symmetric zero-offset DropShadowEffect IS a glow, so BlurRadius
               is Microsoft's 15 rather than ours. The COLOUR is the anachronism: Windows 7 glowed
               white behind black title text; Dark keeps the structure and inverts it. Declared as a
               whole Effect rather than as a Color, because Visual.Effect is an ordinary
               StyledProperty a {DynamicResource} can carry and DropShadowEffect.Color inside a
               ResourceDictionary is not. -->
          <DropShadowEffect x:Key="CaptionTitleGlow" BlurRadius="15" OffsetX="0" OffsetY="0" Color="White" />

          <!-- The three caption buttons' faces. AUTHORED. Aero's own were glossy glass capsules with
               a red close; the alphas below are chosen to read as glass over an arbitrary blurred
               desktop in each variant, and no Windows 7 measurement is claimed for any of them. The
               close button is red AT REST, not only on hover — that is Windows 7's shape, not
               Windows 11's. -->
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonFaceBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#33FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#14FFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonHoverBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#99FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#4DFFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonPressedBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#4D000000" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#26000000" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionCloseFaceBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#66E81123" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#33C50F1F" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionCloseHoverBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#E6E81123" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#B3C50F1F" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
        </ResourceDictionary>

        <!-- ══ Dark ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
             The seven alias lines are BYTE-IDENTICAL to the Light block's: each names an Aero colour
             key, and Themes/Aero/Variant/Dark.axaml is what gives that key its Dark value. They are
             repeated rather than hoisted for the reason this file's header states — the same
             arrangement AeroResources.axaml uses when it merges one Brushes.axaml into both
             dictionaries. Only the keys BELOW them differ, and every difference is either the user's
             accent or an invention this app makes because Windows 7 had no dark mode. -->
        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Dark">
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceBaseBrush"      Color="{DynamicResource AeroWindowBackgroundColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfacePageBrush"      Color="{DynamicResource AeroListBoxBackgroundColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextPrimaryBrush"      Color="{DynamicResource AeroForegroundColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextDisabledBrush"     Color="{DynamicResource AeroDisabledForegroundColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StrokeControlBrush"    Color="{DynamicResource AeroGroupBoxBorderBrushColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="StrokeDividerBrush"    Color="{DynamicResource AeroGroupBoxBorderBrushColor}" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FocusStrokeOuterBrush" Color="{DynamicResource AeroForegroundColor}" />

          <!-- AUTHORED; see the Light block for the measurement that says why, and for what this
               value does and does not achieve. -->
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="HeadingBrush" Color="#6699FF" />

          <Color x:Key="AccentColor">#4CC2FF</Color>
          <Color x:Key="AccentColorShaded">#3E9FD1</Color>

          <!-- The Aero caption, Dark half. See the Light dictionary for every measurement, citation
               and AUTHORED note. WHITE, at half Light's alpha, since Round 7 Task 7 — the black
               ladder Task 1 shipped inverted the band's polarity, and that block carries the
               before/after profile. -->
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassActiveBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#20FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#33FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#1AFFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassInactiveBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#10FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#1AFFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#0DFFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionGlassBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#10FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="0.4" Color="#1AFFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#0DFFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>

          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CaptionBevelLightBrush" Color="#40FFFFFF" />
          <SolidColorBrush x:Key="CaptionBevelDarkBrush"  Color="#66000000" />

          <DropShadowEffect x:Key="CaptionTitleGlow" BlurRadius="15" OffsetX="0" OffsetY="0" Color="Black" />

          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonFaceBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#26FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#0FFFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonHoverBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#66FFFFFF" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#33FFFFFF" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionButtonPressedBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#66000000" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#33000000" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionCloseFaceBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#66E81123" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#33C50F1F" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
          <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="CaptionCloseHoverBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
            <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#E6E81123" />
            <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#B3C50F1F" />
          </LinearGradientBrush>
        </ResourceDictionary>

      </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

      <!-- ══ Variant-independent: shape, spacing, type ═════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <!-- Aero's control radius, and it is an ALIAS rather than a literal 3: AVMAP/Metrics.axaml
           declares AeroControlCornerRadius and every vendored control theme resolves it, so binding
           our own name to theirs is what keeps one number rather than two. The <StaticResource>
           alias form is safe HERE and not in the themed block above, because Metrics.axaml is merged
           OUTSIDE AeroResources.axaml's ThemeDictionaries — a plain parent-stack walk to
           Application.Current finds it and there is no variant to get wrong. Metrics.axaml uses the
           identical form for AeroScrollBarThumbMinimumExtent.

           RadiusPaneTopLeft (8,0,0,0) and RadiusPanel (8) used to be declared beside this key. Round
           7, Task 7 deleted both: Aero's content pane has no rounded corner — the vendored
           ControlStyles/ListBox.axaml sets CornerRadius="0" — and the 应用 page's category panel is a
           group box at this radius now. Their two measurement blocks (the pane's arc scan and the
           four-criterion panel-vs-pane comparison) are archived in
           docs/superpowers/archive/2026-08-04-windows11-measurement-record.md; they were true of the
           Windows 11 build and are not true of this one. -->
      <StaticResource x:Key="RadiusControl" ResourceKey="AeroControlCornerRadius" />

      <!-- RadiusControl MINUS ONE, for a layer that sits just INSIDE a control's own 1px border.
           XAML cannot express "RadiusControl - 1", so this is a second literal, declared immediately
           beside the value it is derived from precisely so the two are changed together. It was 3
           against a RadiusControl of 4; it is 2 against Aero's 3. The vendored layer agrees with the
           derivation rather than merely permitting it: ControlStyles/Button_RepeatButton_ToggleButton
           and ListBox both compute their inner border's radius from the outer one with
           AdjustCornerRadiusConverter, which subtracts exactly 1.

           IT HAD NO {DynamicResource} CONSUMER AT ALL AFTER ROUND 7, TASK 7, AND IT HAS EXACTLY ONE
           NOW — Border.tm-search-affordance in Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml, the search field's
           magnifier/clear face. Task 7 is what emptied it: its two readers were TmTileButton's and
           TmButton's StateLayer Borders, and Aero repaints a button's whole face per state instead of
           washing a translucent layer over it, so there was no inner layer left to round. Task 9 is
           what refilled it, and this remark did not follow either move — it went on naming a
           "0,0,2,2" literal on the search field's focus underline, in Views/AppPage.axaml, for two
           rounds after Task 9 deleted that literal, and then for a third after Round 8, Task 3 moved
           the field itself into MainWindow.axaml. Grepped in the Round 8, Task 3 review: the string
           "0,0,2,2" appears nowhere under src/, and this key's one reader is the Setter named above.
           Corrected rather than deleted, because the reason to KEEP the key is now the ordinary one
           (something reads it) instead of the Space1..Space3 one. -->
      <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusControlInner">2</CornerRadius>

      <!-- ══ Spacing ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           Aero's ladder is PUBLISHED and it is not Fluent's 4/8/12/16/20.
           https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-layout gives, verbatim:
             · "target regions … should either be touching or have at least 3 DLUs (5 relative
                pixels) of space between them", and "label to control 5"          -> Space1 = 5
             · "Between related controls — 4 / 7. Between buttons — 4 / 7"        -> Space2 = 7
             · "Dialog box margins — 7 DLU / 11 relative pixels on all sides",
               "Between unrelated controls — 7 / 11", "Between paragraphs — 11"   -> Space3 = 11
           Space4 (16) and Space5 (20) are DELETED rather than re-valued: the guide publishes nothing
           at either rung, and an unpublished rung is one somebody cites later as if it were measured.
           Where 20 was wanted the replacement is a stated 2 x Space3 = 22 (the 设置 group headings'
           top margin, Launcher.axaml).

           THESE THREE KEYS HAVE NO {DynamicResource} CONSUMER AND ARE NOT MEANT TO. A Margin or a
           Padding is a Thickness and XAML cannot compose one out of a Double resource, so every
           spacing in the tree is written as a literal 5 / 7 / 11 / 22 and these declarations are what
           those literals cite. That was already true of Space1/Space2/Space4/Space5 before this task
           — grep found zero consumers for all four — and it is why the ladder is ALSO enforced by a
           test: SettingsRowTests.NoCardFile_RestatesAConstantTheThemeOwns rejects any Margin, Padding
           or Spacing rung a settings-card file states that is not on this list. SurfacePadding below
           IS a Thickness and does have a consumer. -->
      <x:Double x:Key="Space1">5</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="Space2">7</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="Space3">11</x:Double>
      <!-- The dialog margin, same citation as Space3. Consumed by AppPage's body StackPanel. -->
      <Thickness x:Key="SurfacePadding">11</Thickness>

      <!-- ══ Type ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           vis-fonts: "Segoe UI 9 pt is the system font" — plain Segoe UI, not the Variable optical
           sizes Windows 11 selects by size band. FontFamilySmall / FontFamilyText / FontFamilyDisplay
           were the three optical faces and are deleted; grep found ZERO consumers for two of them
           even before this task. Microsoft YaHei UI stays as the CJK fallback so an item name never
           renders as tofu. Nothing is bundled.

           The sizes are published AND vendored, and the two agree exactly:
             FontSizeBody      9 pt at 96 dpi = 12 DIP    = AeroFontSizeNormal 12   (VENDORED)
             FontSizeSubtitle  main instruction 12 pt = 16 DIP = AeroFontSizeLarge 16 (VENDORED)
             FontSizeGroupHead group headers 11 pt = 14.667 DIP, rounded up to 15    (PUBLISHED)
           FontSizeCaption (12) is deleted: a Windows 7 property sheet uses ONE body size and gets its
           hierarchy from colour and weight. The three LineHeight keys go with it — Windows 11's ramp
           encodes line heights, GDI text does not, and every consumer now takes its natural one. -->
      <FontFamily x:Key="AppFontFamily">Segoe UI, Microsoft YaHei UI</FontFamily>
      <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeBody">12</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeGroupHead">15</x:Double>
      <x:Double x:Key="FontSizeSubtitle">16</x:Double>

      <!-- The icon face is the ONE deliberate Windows 11 anachronism this conversion keeps: Windows 7
           had no icon font at all, its shell glyphs were bitmaps, and hand-drawing fifteen of them is
           not what this round is for. Segoe MDL2 Assets is the Windows 10 fallback at the same code
           points. -->
      <FontFamily x:Key="IconFontFamily">Segoe Fluent Icons, Segoe MDL2 Assets</FontFamily>

      <!-- ══ Overrides that reach Avalonia's own control templates ═════════════════════════════
           These three keys are read with {DynamicResource} by templates this app does NOT replace —
           the PathIcon, the text boxes' MenuFlyout, tooltips, the message box's own Window chrome —
           so setting them here reaches every one of those without touching a template.

           ControlCornerRadius is an ALIAS onto Aero's own radius for the same reason RadiusControl
           is, and it is deliberately NOT the same key as RadiusControl even though both now resolve
           to 3: this one is FluentTheme's key name and reaches Avalonia's templates, ours is the
           app's own shape scale. One name, two audiences, and collapsing them would couple this app's
           card geometry to any future change in Avalonia's popups.

           OverlayCornerRadius is 0, not an alias: Windows 7's popups and flyouts were square, and
           Aero declares no key for them.

           ContentControlThemeFontFamily is LOAD-BEARING and must survive. AVMAP/Metrics.axaml sets
           the same key upstream to a bundled Inter face with NO CJK coverage, which would render this
           app's Chinese item names as tofu. That line is deleted on vendoring AND this override sits
           after Aero in App.axaml's list — belt and braces, plus
           AeroAttributionTests.NoVendoredFile_ReferencesTheInterFontFamily.

           A FOURTH KEY, TitleBarBackgroundBrush, lived here until Round 7, Task 1 deleted it with the
           Fluent-drawn caption it existed to blank; its Round 4 measurement record is preserved in
           docs/superpowers/archive/2026-08-04-windows11-measurement-record.md. -->
      <StaticResource x:Key="ControlCornerRadius" ResourceKey="AeroControlCornerRadius" />
      <CornerRadius x:Key="OverlayCornerRadius">0</CornerRadius>
      <FontFamily x:Key="ContentControlThemeFontFamily">Segoe UI, Microsoft YaHei UI</FontFamily>

    </ResourceDictionary>
  </Styles.Resources>

</Styles>
